\begin_layout Standard
We want to predict weather person C defaults or not,
 to determine if person C should get a credit card.
 Using the euclidean norm,
 we can determine which person,
 person C is mostly identical to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Distance to person A:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dist(A,C)=\sqrt{(47-21)^{2}+(35000-36000)^{2}}\approx1000.34.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Distance to person B:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dist(B,C)=\sqrt{(22-21)^{2}+(40000-36000)^{2}}\approx4000.00.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 when measured in thousands of USD,
 person C looks more like person A (that did not default) than person B (that did default).
 According to this,
 the bank should therefore give person C a credit card.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now let's try to calculate the euclidean norm again.
 This time assuming the income is given in USD:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Distance to person A:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dist(A,C)=\sqrt{(47-21)^{2}+(35-36)^{2}}\approx26.02.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Distance to person B:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dist(B,C)=\sqrt{(22-21)^{2}+(40-36)^{2}}\approx4.12.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This time person C looks more like person B than person A:
 The bank should not give person C a credit card.
 This is because the numerical difference between their incomes are significantly lower,
 when we measure ind USD instead of thousands of USD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Input Centering (9 points)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
(a)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the input matrix:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{X}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\boldsymbol{x}^{T}_{1}\\
\boldsymbol{x}^{T}_{2}\\
\vdots\\
\boldsymbol{x}^{T}_{N}
\end{array}\right],
\]

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 defined as:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\gamma=\boldsymbol{I}-\frac{1}{N}\boldsymbol{11}^{T},
\]

\end_inset

i want to show that:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{Z}=\gamma\boldsymbol{X},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{Z}^{T}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\boldsymbol{z}_{1} & \boldsymbol{z}_{2} & \cdots & \boldsymbol{z}_{N}\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{z}_{n}=\boldsymbol{x}_{n}-\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Firstly i rewrite 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{Z}$
\end_inset

,
 so it is expressed only in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{X}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{1}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{Z}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\boldsymbol{x}^{T}_{1}\\
\boldsymbol{x}^{T}_{2}\\
\vdots\\
\boldsymbol{x}^{T}_{N}
\end{array}\right]-\left[\begin{array}{c}
\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{T}\\
\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{T}\\
\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{T}\\
\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}^{T}
\end{array}\right],
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\boldsymbol{X}-\boldsymbol{1\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}}^{T},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\boldsymbol{X}-\boldsymbol{1}\frac{1}{N}\boldsymbol{1}^{T}\boldsymbol{X},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\boldsymbol{X}-\frac{1}{N}\boldsymbol{11}^{T}\boldsymbol{X}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This means,
 that i need to show:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{Z}=\gamma\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{X}-\frac{1}{N}\boldsymbol{11}^{T}\boldsymbol{X}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting in the expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

,
 i get:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{Z}=\left(\boldsymbol{I}-\frac{1}{N}\boldsymbol{11}^{T}\right)\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{X}-\frac{1}{N}\boldsymbol{11}^{T}\boldsymbol{X}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The expression 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{X}-\frac{1}{N}\boldsymbol{11}^{T}\boldsymbol{X}$
\end_inset

 factorizes to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\boldsymbol{I}-\frac{1}{N}\boldsymbol{11}^{T}\right)\boldsymbol{X}$
\end_inset

,
 meaning that the equality shown above is true.
 Thus,
 i have shown that:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{Z}=\gamma\boldsymbol{X}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
(b)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To determine that 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{rank}(\boldsymbol{Z})<N$
\end_inset

,
 i use that the rank of the columns and the rank of the rows are equal.
 Consider the rank of the rows in the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{Z}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{Z}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\boldsymbol{z}^{T}_{1}\\
\boldsymbol{z}^{T}_{2}\\
\vdots\\
\boldsymbol{z}^{T}_{N}
\end{array}\right].
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{z}_{n}$
\end_inset

is constructed as the deviations of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}_{n}$
\end_inset

from the mean,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{z}_{n}=\boldsymbol{x}_{n}-\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}$
\end_inset

,
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 rows of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{Z}$
\end_inset

 must be linearly dependent:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum^{N}_{n=1}\left(\boldsymbol{x}_{n}-\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\boldsymbol{x}_{n}\right)=\sum^{N}_{n=1}\boldsymbol{x}_{n}-N\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\boldsymbol{x}_{n},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\sum^{N}_{n=1}\boldsymbol{x}_{n}-\sum^{N}_{n=1}\boldsymbol{x}_{n},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since at least the 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 rows of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{Z}$
\end_inset

 are linearly dependent,
 the rank of the rows in 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{Z}$
\end_inset

 must be smaller than 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 (the rank is the maximum number of linearly independent rows).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Input Whitening (18 points)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
(a)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 is 1 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{Var}(\hat{x}_{1})=1$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=\hat{x}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 The variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 is given by:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Var}(x_{2})=\text{Var}\left(\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\hat{x}_{1}+\epsilon\hat{x}_{2}\right),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\text{Var}\left(\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\hat{x}_{1}\right)+\text{Var}(\epsilon\hat{x}_{2})+2\text{Cov}\left(\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\hat{x}_{1},\epsilon\hat{x}_{2}\right).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{2}$
\end_inset

 are independent,
 the covariance is 0,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{Cov}\left(\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\hat{x}_{1},\epsilon\hat{x}_{2}\right)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Using this gives me:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Var}(x_{2})=\text{Var}\left(\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\hat{x}_{1}\right)+\text{Var}(\epsilon\hat{x}_{2}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{Var}(bX)=b^{2}\text{Var}(X)$
\end_inset

,
 we can rewrite the expression:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Var}(x_{2})=\left(\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\right)^{2}\text{Var}(\hat{x}_{1})+\epsilon^{2}\text{Var}(\hat{x}_{2}),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=(1-\epsilon^{2})\text{Var}(\hat{x}_{1})+\epsilon^{2}\text{Var}(\hat{x}_{2}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I know that 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{Var}(\hat{x}_{1})=\text{Var}(\hat{x}_{2})=1$
\end_inset

.
 Using this yields the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Var}(x_{2})=1-\epsilon^{2}+\epsilon^{2},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Var}(x_{2})=1.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To calculate the covariance of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 i use the following formula:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Cov}(X,Y)=E(XY)-E(X)E(Y).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the formula yields:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Cov}(x_{1},x_{2})=E\left(\hat{x}_{1}\cdot\left(\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\hat{x}_{1}+\epsilon\hat{x}_{2}\right)\right)-E(\hat{x}_{1})E\left(\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\hat{x}_{1}+\epsilon\hat{x}_{2}\right).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since both 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{2}$
\end_inset

 have zero mean,
 we get:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Cov}(x_{1},x_{2})=E\left(\hat{x}_{1}\cdot\left(\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\hat{x}_{1}+\epsilon\hat{x}_{2}\right)\right),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=E\left(\hat{x}^{2}_{1}\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}+\hat{x}_{2}\hat{x}_{1}\epsilon\right),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}E(\hat{x}^{2}_{1})+\epsilon E(\hat{x}_{1}\hat{x}_{2}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\hat{x}_{1})=0,$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $E(\hat{x}^{2}_{1})$
\end_inset

 can be rewritten to 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\hat{x}^{2}_{1})=E(\hat{x}^{2}_{1})-E(\hat{x}_{1})^{2}=\text{Var}(\hat{x}_{1})=1$
\end_inset

,
 which yields:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Cov}(x_{1},x_{2})=\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}+\epsilon E(\hat{x}_{2}\hat{x}_{1}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{2}$
\end_inset

 are independent both with zero mean,
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\hat{x}_{1}\hat{x}_{2})$
\end_inset

 can be rewritten to 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\hat{x}_{1}\hat{x}_{2})=E(\hat{x}_{1})E(\hat{x}_{2})=0$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Cov}(x_{1},x_{2})=\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
(b)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $(w_{1},w_{2})$
\end_inset

 as functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{w}_{1},\hat{w}_{2})$
\end_inset

 i start by plugging the expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}$
\end_inset

 into the expressions for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 and rewrite 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{2}$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{2}=\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}x_{1}+\epsilon\hat{x}_{2},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Leftrightarrow\epsilon\hat{x}_{2}=x_{2}-\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}x_{1},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Leftrightarrow\hat{x}_{2}=\frac{x_{2}-\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}x_{1}}{\epsilon}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next i plug the above expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{2}$
\end_inset

 and the expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}$
\end_inset

 into the function 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}})$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}})=\hat{w}_{1}\hat{x}_{1}+\hat{w}_{2}\hat{x}_{2},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Leftrightarrow f(\boldsymbol{x})=\hat{w}_{1}x_{1}+\hat{w}_{2}\frac{x_{2}-\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}x_{1}}{\epsilon},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\left(\hat{w}_{1}-\hat{w}_{2}\frac{\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}}{\epsilon}\right)x_{1}+\hat{w}_{2}\frac{x_{2}}{\epsilon},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\left(\hat{w}_{1}-\hat{w}_{2}\frac{\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}}{\epsilon}\right)x_{1}+\frac{\hat{w}_{2}}{\epsilon}x_{2}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the expression above we can infer 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}(\hat{w}_{1},\hat{w}_{2})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}(\hat{w}_{2})$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{1}(\hat{w}_{1},\hat{w}_{2})=\hat{w}_{1}-\hat{w}_{2}\frac{\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}}{\epsilon},
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{2}(\hat{w}_{2})=\frac{\hat{w}_{2}}{\epsilon}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
(c)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the two-norm regularization with the restricted hypothesis space:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{H}_{C}=\{f(\boldsymbol{x})=w_{1}x_{1}+w_{2}x_{2}|\boldsymbol{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}\wedge w^{2}_{1}+w^{2}_{2}\leq C\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the minimum value of 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

,
 i first plug in the expression of 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}$
\end_inset

from above into the regularization constraint:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w^{2}_{1}+w^{2}_{2}\leq C,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Leftrightarrow\left(\hat{w}_{1}-\hat{w}_{2}\frac{\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}}{\epsilon}\right)^{2}+\left(\frac{\hat{w}_{2}}{\epsilon}\right)^{2}\leq C,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Leftrightarrow\hat{w}^{2}_{1}+\left(\hat{w}_{2}\frac{\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}}{\epsilon}\right)^{2}-2\hat{w}_{1}\hat{w}_{2}\frac{\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}}{\epsilon}+\frac{\hat{w}^{2}_{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}\leq C,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Leftrightarrow\hat{w}^{2}_{1}+\hat{w}^{2}_{2}\frac{1-\epsilon^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}-2\hat{w}_{1}\hat{w}_{2}\frac{\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}}{\epsilon}+\frac{\hat{w}^{2}_{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}\leq C,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Leftrightarrow\hat{w}^{2}_{1}+\hat{w}^{2}_{2}\frac{2-\epsilon^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}-2\hat{w}_{1}\hat{w}_{2}\frac{\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}}{\epsilon}\leq C.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The target function 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\hat{\boldsymbol{x}})$
\end_inset

 has 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{1}=\hat{w}_{2}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Substituting this in yields:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1+\frac{2-\epsilon^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}-2\frac{\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}}{\epsilon}\leq C,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{2}{\epsilon^{2}}-2\frac{\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}}{\epsilon}\leq C,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{2}{\epsilon^{2}}\left(1-\epsilon\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\right)\leq C.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus we get the minimum value of 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 (the maximum amount of regularization):
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C=\frac{2}{\epsilon^{2}}\left(1-\epsilon\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\right).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
(d)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
when the correlation increases (
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

),
 the first part of the expression increases infinitely 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\frac{2}{\epsilon^{2}}\rightarrow\infty\right)$
\end_inset

,
 and the second part converges ton 1 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1-\epsilon\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\rightarrow1,\ \text{as}\ \epsilon\sqrt{1-\epsilon^{2}}\rightarrow0\right)$
\end_inset

.
 This means that the minimum value of 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 increases 
\begin_inset Formula $(C\rightarrow\infty)$
\end_inset

 as the correlation increases 
\begin_inset Formula $(\epsilon\rightarrow0)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Hoeffding's Bound (15 points)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To calculate the probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{n}>22$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $n=100$
\end_inset

,
 i use the Hoffdings' inequality:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(S_{n}-E[S_{n}]\geq\varepsilon\right)\leq\exp\left(-\frac{2\varepsilon{}^{2}}{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}\right).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 takes the values 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}\in\{-2,0.8,1\}$
\end_inset

.
 The lower bound is 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}=-2$
\end_inset

,
 and the upper bound is 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}=1$
\end_inset

.
 The expected value 
\begin_inset Formula $E[S_{100}]$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[S_{100}]=100E[X_{i}]=100\cdot1/3(-2+0.8+1)=-100\frac{0.2}{3}=-\frac{20}{3}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This yields 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varepsilon=22-E[S_{100}]=22-\left(-\frac{20}{3}\right)=\frac{86}{3}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I can now calculate the probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{n}>22$
\end_inset

 using the inequality:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}(S_{100}>22)\leq\mathbb{P}\left(S_{100}-E[S_{100}]\geq\frac{86}{3}\right)\leq\exp\left(-\frac{2\cdot\left(\frac{86}{3}\right){}^{2}}{100\left(1-(-2)\right)^{2}}\right),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Leftrightarrow\mathbb{P}(S>22)\lesssim\exp(-1.8262)\approx0.1610.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 the probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{n}>22$
\end_inset

 is at most 
\begin_inset Formula $\approx16.1\%$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

